> "Figure X showcases the projected change in the Gini coefficient by 2050 under two contrasting scenarios: 'No Climate Policy' and 'Paris Agreement with equal per capita (EPC) carbon revenue redistribution.' The 'No Climate Policy' scenario highlights a stark macroeconomic risk, with climate impacts expected to increase inequality by an average of 1.4 points in the Gini index. Such an increase threatens fiscal stability and social equity, especially across low- and middle-income countries. In contrast, the 'Paris Agreement with EPC' scenario demonstrates how well-designed adaptation policies can mitigate these risks. By redistributing carbon revenues equally among citizens, this approach not only offsets the temporary inequality increase due to climate policies but also lowers the Gini index by nearly two points in the short term. These findings derive from a comprehensive synthesis across eight diverse integrated assessment models, reinforcing the robustness and reliability of these insights for guiding fiscal planning and inclusive policy design." 
